"Imagine you’re responsible for a critical S3 bucket that stores customer documents.
One of your teammates accidentally runs a script that deletes a folder. What would you do?"

This wasn’t about Lambda, Kubernetes, or CI/CD pipelines.
It was about protecting data—and honestly, it’s one of the most important DevOps responsibilities.

𝐇𝐨𝐰 𝐈 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐰𝐞𝐫𝐞𝐝:

• "First, I’d check if S3 versioning was enabled. If yes, recovery is simple—I’d restore the previous versions of the deleted objects."

• "If versioning wasn’t enabled, that’s where things get tricky. I’d check for backups—maybe AWS Backup, cross-region replication, or third-party snapshots."

• "For the long term, I’d make sure S3 bucket policies are configured properly. Use Object Lock (WORM) for critical data if needed, and restrict delete permissions using IAM policies."

• "Also, I’d enable event notifications for delete operations so we’re instantly alerted next time something like this happens."

𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐈𝐧𝐭𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐢𝐞𝐰𝐞𝐫 𝐒𝐚𝐢𝐝:
"I like that you’re thinking beyond the immediate fix. Prevention is just as important as recovery."

In DevOps, it’s not just about uptime.
It’s about ensuring your systems—and your data—are resilient, recoverable, and secure.
